Here's the upload order and the prompt. For a multi-element composite like this, the rule is canvas/background first, then the elements in the order you'll reference them, then the style reference last.

Upload in this order:

@Image1 = fiery background (your plain flame image, no text)

@Image2 = Chakalaka pouch

@Image3 = Langer Pfeffer pouch

@Image4 = Bird's Eye Chili pouch

@Image5 = Chief Guest title card (font/art-style reference only)

Use @Image1 as the background canvas. Place the three product pouches in a row, evenly spaced, standing upright: @Image2 on the left, @Image3 in the center, @Image4 on the right. Keep each pouch's label, colors, and shape exactly as in its reference image.

Below each pouch, draw small flame-shaped chili icons in the hand-drawn flame art style of @Image1: one flame under @Image2, two flames under @Image3, three flames under @Image4.

At the top, add the German text "WÄHLE DEINEN SCHÄRFEGRAD" in the exact bold comic font, white and yellow coloring, and heavy outline style of the title text in @Image5.

Match the lighting and flame art style across the whole image so the products sit naturally in the scene.

A few things built into this on purpose:

The spice ordering follows what you said, not the packs. You asked for Chakalaka = least (1), Langer Pfeffer = middle (2), Bird's Eye = hottest (3). Worth knowing: the actual Schärfegrad dots on the packs read Chakalaka 2/5 and Bird's Eye 4/5 — so your 1-2-3 game scale is your own creative choice, which is fine, just flagging it isn't the label data.

"WÄHLE DEINEN SCHÄRFEGRAD" is the natural German for "choose your spice level," and it matches the word Schärfegrad already printed on your own pouches — so the interface text and product labels stay consistent.

@Image5 is style-only. The prompt uses it for font and outline treatment, not content — you don't want the cartoon man or "Chief Guest" bleeding in. If any of that leaks into the result, add one line: "Do not include any person or any text from @Image5 — use it only for font style."

One choice to make before you run it: do you want the chili icons drawn fresh in the flame style (what I wrote), or do you have a specific chili icon you'd rather I reference? Drawn-fresh keeps the art unified, but if you have a set icon, add it as @Image6 and I'll point the prompt at it instead.
